1. PROJECT OVERVIEW & REAL-WORLD SCENARIO
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
During high-demand events—such as an IRCTC Tatkal train booking window at 10:00 AM
or a cold-play concert flash sale—thousands of users attempt to book a limited
number of seats at the exact same millisecond.

If the backend server does not manage concurrent access properly, three fatal
real-world bugs occur:
  1. Double Booking: Two users pay for and receive confirmation for Seat "A1".
  2. Ghost Locks: A user selects Seat "A2", their internet disconnects during
     payment, and Seat "A2" remains permanently locked so nobody else can buy it.
  3. Unfair Waitlists: When a confirmed user cancels a ticket, the seat is
     grabbed randomly instead of going to the first person waiting in line.

Your objective is to build a pure Core Java backend engine that simulates a
coach of seats, handles dozens of simultaneous buyer threads, enforces temporary
seat hold timers, recovers gracefully from payment failures using custom
exceptions, and automatically promotes waitlisted users upon cancellation.


